  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nOther than locally dense valley fog tonight the weather will be\nfair. Radiational cooling will bring temperatures down into the\n50s in outlying areas, with a few spots in northern NH possibly\nreaching the upper 40s. Have heavily blended in the MET and MAV\nguidance for lows.\n\nA weak short wave trough will approach northern New England\nWednesday. This feature coupled with at least weak instability\nshould allow for widely scattered showers and isolated\nthunderstorms during the afternoon hours. With SFC dewpoint\ndepressions around 20F gusty winds could occur in convective\nshowers and storms. However, coverage will be widely scattered\nand most locations will likely remain dry. Valley fog will be\nlikely once again late at night.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nA weak trough axis swings into the area Thursday afternoon and \nis likely to persist through Friday. During its passage, showers\nand storms are possible, but PWATs in the range of 1.1-1.5 \ninches should allow for showers to be fairly scattered rather \nthan more widespread.\n\nIn addition, we will need to keep an eye on the high tides for \nThursday. Astronomical tides are high for the month, and the tide \ngauges will be close to reaching minor flood stage. However, light \noffshore winds and very low wave heights could allow for some high \nwater along the coast, but otherwise impacts should be minimal.\n\nOver the weekend, a mid-level ridge moves into the area. Ridging\nand high pressure should allow for dry and pleasant weather,\nwith high temperatures forecast near 80F.",
